---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';

/** The loop, start to finish. Judgment is not a stage — it runs across all five. */
const loop = [
  ['Frame the intent', 'Define what a good result has to be true of'],
  ['Generate working options', 'Build them, do not describe them'],
  ['Review in context', 'Put running software in front of the people who know different parts of the problem'],
  ['Decide together', 'Rank, choose, and say why'],
  ['Strengthen the system', 'Fold the decision back into what everything else is generated from'],
];

/** What a brief has to carry before anything is generated. */
const brief = [
  ['Intent', 'Who is trying to do what, and which decision should get easier'],
  ['Required behavior', 'States, transitions, permissions, failure paths'],
  ['System constraints', 'The tokens, components and patterns the work must resolve to'],
  ['Prohibited actions', 'What the agent may not do without a person'],
  ['Open questions', 'What the work should help us resolve'],
  ['Evaluation criteria', 'How we will judge the working experience'],
];

/** Design intent becomes an interface through the system, not around it. */
const encode = [
  ['Design intent', 'The behavior and hierarchy the work has to produce'],
  ['Tokens', 'Colour, type, spacing, radius — the approved values'],
  ['Components', 'The patterns already agreed, and their documented states'],
  ['Behavioral rules', 'Accessibility minimums and brand rules, as constraints rather than notes'],
  ['Working interface', 'Generated inside all four, not compared against them afterwards'],
];

/** The loop that finds a misunderstanding while it is still cheap. */
const collaborate = [
  ['Frame', 'State the intended outcome'],
  ['Generate', 'Build the smallest working expression of it'],
  ['Review', 'Put it in front of people who understand different parts of the problem'],
  ['Find the mismatch', 'Where do the mental models diverge?'],
  ['Correct both', 'The implementation, and the brief and system that produced it'],
];

/** Six lenses, one question each. */
const lenses = [
  ['Intent', 'Does this solve the problem we agreed to solve?'],
  ['Behavior', 'Do the states, transitions, permissions and failure paths make sense?'],
  ['System', 'Does this strengthen the product’s patterns, or erode them?'],
  ['Content', 'Does the interface explain what happened and what to do next?'],
  ['Accessibility', 'Can people perceive, understand and operate it in real conditions?'],
  ['Implementation', 'Is the behavior credible in the actual product environment?'],
];

const mayProceed = [
  'Generate options inside the existing system',
  'Implement approved components and patterns',
  'Run checks and report failures',
  'Document its decisions',
  'Propose a system addition when no approved pattern exists',
];

const needsAPerson = [
  'Deploy to production',
  'Delete or migrate consequential data',
  'Restructure the product architecture',
  'Invent design-system values',
  'Conceal a failed check',
  'Resolve meaningful product ambiguity by assumption',
  'Treat a technically valid result as product approval',
];

/** Where leverage comes from now. */
const leverage = [
  'The clarity of the product intent',
  'The strength of the design system',
  'The quality of the evaluation criteria',
  'The boundaries around autonomous action',
  'The speed and honesty of the review loop',
  'The team’s ability to turn individual learning into shared infrastructure',
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I make design intent executable"
  metaTitle="I make design intent executable | Tyler Young"
  description="AI lets me move from an idea to running software without surrendering the judgment, systems thinking, or collaboration that make the work good. The operating model behind that practice."
  eyebrow="Operating model"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Practice', 'AI-assisted product design'],
    ['My role', 'Frame · direct · evaluate · align'],
    ['Output', 'Working options, production-ready systems, clearer decisions'],
    ['Principle', 'The machine increases range. People remain responsible for meaning and quality.'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    AI lets me move from an idea to running software without surrendering the
    judgment, systems thinking, or collaboration that make the work good.
  </p>

  <p class="sr-only">
    The operating loop runs in five stages. Frame the intent: define what a good
    result has to be true of. Generate working options: build them rather than
    describe them. Review in context: put running software in front of the people
    who know different parts of the problem. Decide together: rank, choose, and
    say why. Strengthen the system: fold the decision back into what everything
    else is generated from. Human judgment runs across all five stages rather
    than sitting at the end as a gate.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop" aria-hidden="true">
    {
      loop.map(([actor, what]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{actor}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{what}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p class="note-line">Human judgment runs across all five, not at the end.</p>

  <p>AI changed the distance between design intent and working software.</p>
  <p>
    I can explore several directions, test them in the actual interface, and
    return to a product conversation with something people can use — not only
    something they can imagine from a static artifact.
  </p>
  <p>That changes my medium. It does not change my responsibility.</p>
  <p>
    My job is still to understand the problem, frame the right behavior, protect
    the coherence of the product, bring the right people into the decision, and
    know when an attractive result is solving the wrong thing. I use AI to make
    more of that thinking executable.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Frame</p>
  <h2>I begin with intent, not instructions for a picture</h2>
  <p>
    An agent can build a polished version of a poorly framed idea remarkably
    quickly. So before generating anything, I define the conditions of a good
    result:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Who is trying to accomplish what?</li>
    <li>What decision should become easier?</li>
    <li>What must remain true across every state?</li>
    <li>What can the system do autonomously?</li>
    <li>What requires confirmation or human judgment?</li>
    <li>What would make the result wrong, even if it looked finished?</li>
    <li>How will we evaluate the working experience?</li>
  </ul>

  <ul class="cells three">
    {
      brief.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    This is not less design specification. It is a more complete specification of
    the experience — behavior, hierarchy, states, content, accessibility, system
    constraints, and the questions the work should help us answer.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    The quality of generated work is limited by the quality of the judgment
    encoded around it.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Encode</p>
  <h2>The design system becomes part of the production environment</h2>
  <p>
    Every colour, type style, spacing value, radius, interaction pattern and
    accessibility rule should resolve to the system. If a required value or
    pattern does not exist, the agent should name the gap rather than quietly
    invent an exception.
  </p>
  <p>
    That changes what a design system is. It stops being documentation people
    consult, or a standard applied at review, and becomes part of the environment
    the interface is generated from.
  </p>

  <p class="sr-only">
    Design intent, tokens, components and behavioral rules together produce the
    working interface: the interface is generated inside all four rather than
    compared against them afterwards.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop" aria-hidden="true">
    {
      encode.map(([actor, what]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{actor}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{what}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    At Atheer I rebuilt an ageing system into a pipeline that worked this way.
    Explorations came back as working components, and approved decisions moved
    toward engineering as code rather than a secondhand reading of redlines.
  </p>
  <p>The leverage ran in both directions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Designers could explore without abandoning product coherence.</li>
    <li>Engineers received behavior expressed in the implementation medium.</li>
    <li>Accessibility and brand rules were present before review, not after it.</li>
    <li>A new decision could strengthen the shared system instead of becoming an isolated exception.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/ops-manager">The product and the system it was built from</a> are both
    running, and the component library documents the states that prove it.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    A mature design system does more than describe the product. It constrains
    what the product is allowed to become.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Explore</p>
  <h2>Options come back as working software</h2>
  <p>
    AI makes producing alternatives inexpensive. It does not make choosing among
    them automatic.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a recent engagement I directed three approaches to the same problem and
    deployed each to its own URL. We evaluated actual behavior, content,
    responsiveness and edge cases rather than debating static approximations.
  </p>
  <p>
    That changes the quality of the conversation. A stakeholder can try the
    workflow. An engineer can see a structural problem. A designer can compare
    hierarchy under real content. The team can discover that two people read the
    same requirement differently — before either reading hardens into production
    work.
  </p>
  <p>
    I still read the options, rank them, and explain why. Generation creates
    possibilities; design judgment creates direction.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    Exploration is cheaper. Deciding what deserves to exist is not.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Collaborate</p>
  <h2>Running software gives the team something precise to disagree about</h2>
  <p>No specification captures everything people mean.</p>
  <p>
    A designer may use a word differently from an engineer. A product manager may
    assume an edge case is included. An agent may satisfy the literal instruction
    and miss the reason behind it. Working software makes those misunderstandings
    observable.
  </p>

  <p class="sr-only">
    The collaboration loop runs in five steps: frame the intended outcome;
    generate the smallest working expression of it; review it with people who
    understand different parts of the problem; find where the mental models
    diverge; then correct both the implementation and the brief and system that
    produced it.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop" aria-hidden="true">
    {
      collaborate.map(([actor, what]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{actor}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{what}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    Human collaboration is not a concession to unreliable AI. It has always been
    how complex product decisions become shared and correct. AI simply makes the
    object under discussion arrive sooner.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/review-loop">A stakeholder review run this way</a> is on the site: two
    running builds, forty-eight notes, and the three that show where the human
    work actually was.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    The model can expose an interpretation. Only the team can decide whether it
    understood the intent.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Evaluate</p>
  <h2>I review the reasoning the interface expresses</h2>
  <p>A generated screen can be clean, consistent, and still wrong.</p>

  <ul class="cells three">
    {
      lenses.map(([label, q]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{q}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    This is where design leadership stays close to the work. I do not need to
    draw every artifact by hand to hold a bar, but I do need to recognise where
    the work is coherent, where it is merely plausible, and where the system
    underneath it needs to improve.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Boundaries</p>
  <h2>Autonomy needs an explicit perimeter</h2>
  <p>I define what an agent may do on its own, and what requires a person.</p>

  <h3>May proceed</h3>
  <ul>{mayProceed.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>

  <h3>Requires a person</h3>
  <ul>{needsAPerson.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>

  <p>
    These boundaries are not there because I expect the agent to behave like a
    bad actor. They exist because speed magnifies misunderstandings. The goal is
    not maximum autonomy; it is useful autonomy, with visible consequences and
    clear accountability.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    I automate execution more readily than I automate judgment.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Accountability</p>
  <h2>Code can be generated. Accountability cannot.</h2>
  <p>Anything that ships still belongs to people.</p>
  <p>
    Whoever approves the work needs to understand what changed, which checks ran,
    where uncertainty remains, and what could be affected. A human name on a
    change should mean informed responsibility, not a ritual click after an
    automated process.
  </p>
  <p>
    That principle connects my product work to my own practice. In
    <a class="link" href="/alucify">Alucify</a> I designed the surfaces that expose coverage gaps,
    downstream impact, stale analysis, and the places where human interpretation
    is still required. I hold my own work to the same rule: automation should
    make its output inspectable and its limits explicit.
  </p>
  <p>
    I do not ask whether an agent is trustworthy in the abstract. I ask whether
    this result is grounded, reviewable, and safe to advance.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    The useful form of trust is not confidence in the machine. It is evidence a
    person can inspect.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Leadership</p>
  <h2>AI changes the leverage of a design leader</h2>
  <p>
    Design leadership used to scale mostly through capacity: how much a team
    could research, draw, review, document and shepherd through implementation.
    Capacity still matters, but it is no longer the only constraint.
  </p>
  <p>Increasingly the leverage is in the environment the work comes out of:</p>
  <ul>{leverage.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>
    That lets me stay close to consequential decisions without becoming the
    production bottleneck, and lets a team explore more widely while holding one
    standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    The opportunity is not to replace designers with agents. It is to remove
    avoidable production friction so designers spend more time understanding
    problems, comparing possibilities, resolving ambiguity, and improving the
    systems that shape the next piece of work.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    My leverage comes from improving the system that produces the work, not from
    increasing the volume of work produced.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">Closing</p>
  <h2>The systems designers own now compile</h2>
  <p>
    I am not presenting myself as a frontend engineer, and I do not think every
    designer needs to become one. I am describing a change in the material of
    design.
  </p>
  <p>
    Intent can become behavior sooner. Design systems can govern generated
    implementation. Options can be evaluated as running software. Review can
    happen before a misunderstanding becomes expensive. Decisions can reach
    production with less translation between the people who made them and the
    people who build them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tools are new. The standard is not. The work still has to be useful,
    coherent, accessible, explainable, and right for the people it serves.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    AI helps me make design executable. Human judgment decides whether it is
    worth shipping.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
